A simple test to see whether inverter detects an amplitude boost due to oil in the reservoir.
(Examples are on intel linux, so you may not need the suswapbytes pipes)

# The demos here use bigendian data. SU takes this directly if compiled with SUXDR on (I recommend this)
# if your SU install is not SUXDR and little endian, insert suswapbytes at suitable pipes (|) below

# Synthetic has been created with...
delivery ModelDescription.xml -m prior_traces.su -v 3 -PD -II -IS -p --IF 1 --make-synthetic 40 4 1.8 2.2
cp near_stack_synth.su near-stack.su

# This is what prior-drawn brine synthetics look like, compared to the 'truth'......
delivery ModelDescription.xml -m prior_traces.su -v 3 -PD -II -IS -N 50 --IF 0 -S 1.8 2.2
cat near-stack.su near_stack_synth.su  | suxgraph linewidth=5,1 reverse=1


# This is what prior-drawn oil synthetics look like, compared to the 'truth'......
delivery ModelDescription.xml -m prior_traces.su -v 3 -PD -II -IS -N 50 --IF 1 -S 1.8 2.2
cat near-stack.su near_stack_synth.su  | suxgraph linewidth=5,1 reverse=1

# The boost means we expect to see very small posterior brine probability (a few %). To check:
delivery ModelDescription.xml -m prior_traces.su -v 3 -PD -II -RWS -N 500


# Whereas, is you ignore seismic (-IS), the posterior odds are still 50:50, which is what the prior specified...
delivery ModelDescription.xml -m prior_traces.su -v 3 -PD -II -IS -RWS -N 500
